  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Consider the list of decimal digits from zero to nine that are multiples of three. If the order of the digits is not important, in how many ways can we choose two different digits from the list?",
  "worked_solution": "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $4$ digits that are multiples of three ($0,3,6,9$), so $n=4.$ We want to select $2$ of those digits, so we have $r=2.$\n\nTherefore, the number of choices is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{4}{C}_{2} & =\\frac{4!}{(4-2)!\\,2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{4!}{2!2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 3\\cdot \\cancel{2!}}{2!\\cancel{2!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 3}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{2} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
